<body>

    <h1>Terminal Bat 🦇</h1>

    <p>
        A small Python terminal animation that turns a CSS pixel-art bat
        into an animated Unicode Braille sprite.
    </p>

    <p>
        The bat can fly across the terminal, return, perch, swarm with friends,
        or perform a tiny dance.
    </p>

    <p>
        The project started as an experiment in converting a CSS
        <code>box-shadow</code> pixel animation into something that could be
        rendered directly in a terminal.
    </p>


    <h2>Features</h2>

    <ul>
        <li>Parses the original SCSS animation frames directly</li>
        <li>Converts pixel coordinates into Unicode Braille characters</li>
        <li>Supports ANSI true-color terminal output</li>
        <li>Clips the sprite correctly while entering and leaving the screen</li>
        <li>Automatically adapts to the current terminal size</li>
        <li>Works from PowerShell and Bash / WSL / Linux</li>
        <li>Includes multiple animation modes</li>
    </ul>


    <h2>Modes</h2>

    <p>Run the default animation:</p>

    <pre><code>bat</code></pre>

    <p>Available modes:</p>

    <pre><code>bat --return
bat --perch
bat --swarm
bat --dance</code></pre>


    <h3><code>bat</code></h3>

    <p>
        The standard animation. The bat flies in from the left and exits
        through the right side of the terminal.
    </p>


    <h3><code>bat --return</code></h3>

    <p>
        The bat flies across the terminal, turns around, and flies back.
    </p>


    <h3><code>bat --perch</code></h3>

    <p>
        The bat flies into the terminal and stays there flapping.
    </p>

    <p>Press:</p>

    <pre><code>Ctrl+C</code></pre>

    <p>to release it.</p>


    <h3><code>bat --swarm</code></h3>

    <p>
        Sends five smaller bats across the terminal using different heights,
        starting positions, and animation phases.
    </p>


    <h3><code>bat --dance</code></h3>

    <p>
        The bat flies to the center of the terminal, performs a small
        side-to-side dance with a vertical bob, and then flies away.
    </p>


    <hr>


    <h2>Requirements</h2>

    <ul>
        <li>Python 3</li>
        <li>A terminal with ANSI escape-sequence support</li>
        <li>Unicode / Braille character support</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        The Python implementation uses only the standard library, so no
        additional Python packages are required.
    </p>


    <hr>


    <h2>Running Directly</h2>

    <p>
        Clone the repository and enter the project directory.
    </p>

    <p>Then run:</p>

    <pre><code>python3 bat.py</code></pre>

    <p>Or choose a mode:</p>

    <pre><code>python3 bat.py --dance
python3 bat.py --return
python3 bat.py --perch
python3 bat.py --swarm</code></pre>

    <p>
        On systems where Python is invoked using <code>python</code> rather
        than <code>python3</code>, use:
    </p>

    <pre><code>python bat.py</code></pre>


    <hr>


    <h2>Shell Setup</h2>

    <p>
        The repository contains example shell snippets inside:
    </p>

    <pre><code>shell/
├── bashrc_snippet.sh
└── powershell_profile.ps1</code></pre>

    <p>
        These allow the program to be called simply as:
    </p>

    <pre><code>bat</code></pre>

    <p>from anywhere in the terminal.</p>


    <h3>PowerShell</h3>

    <p>
        Add the function from:
    </p>

    <pre><code>shell/powershell_profile.ps1</code></pre>

    <p>
        to your PowerShell profile.
    </p>

    <p>
        You can open the profile with:
    </p>

    <pre><code>notepad $PROFILE</code></pre>

    <p>
        or your preferred editor.
    </p>

    <p>
        Replace the example path with the actual path to
        <code>bat.py</code>:
    </p>

    <pre><code>function bat {
    param(
        [Parameter(ValueFromRemainingArguments = $true)]
        [string[]]$BatArgs
    )

    python3 "C:\path\to\bat_animation\bat.py" @BatArgs
}</code></pre>

    <p>Reload the profile:</p>

    <pre><code>. $PROFILE</code></pre>

    <p>You can now use:</p>

    <pre><code>bat
bat --dance
bat --return
bat --perch
bat --swarm</code></pre>


    <h4>Optional PowerShell Startup Animation</h4>

    <p>
        To make the bat fly whenever PowerShell starts, add:
    </p>

    <pre><code>bat</code></pre>

    <p>
        after the function definition in your profile.
    </p>


    <h3>Bash / Linux / WSL</h3>

    <p>
        Add the function from:
    </p>

    <pre><code>shell/bashrc_snippet.sh</code></pre>

    <p>to:</p>

    <pre><code>~/.bashrc</code></pre>

    <p>
        and replace the example path with the location of
        <code>bat.py</code>:
    </p>

    <pre><code>bat() {
    python3 /path/to/bat_animation/bat.py "$@"
}</code></pre>

    <p>Reload Bash:</p>

    <pre><code>source ~/.bashrc</code></pre>

    <p>You can then use:</p>

    <pre><code>bat
bat --dance
bat --return
bat --perch
bat --swarm</code></pre>

    <p>
        The same function works with Bash on both Linux and WSL.
    </p>

    <p>
        For WSL, a project stored on the Windows <code>C:</code> drive may
        have a path resembling:
    </p>

    <pre><code>/mnt/c/Users/username/path/to/bat_animation/bat.py</code></pre>


    <h4>Optional Bash Startup Animation</h4>

    <p>
        To run the animation whenever a new Bash shell starts:
    </p>

    <pre><code>if [[ -z "$BAT_STARTUP_DONE" ]]; then
    BAT_STARTUP_DONE=1
    bat
fi</code></pre>

    <p>
        The guard prevents the animation from running again when
        <code>.bashrc</code> is manually reloaded in the same shell.
    </p>


    <hr>


    <h2>Project Structure</h2>

    <pre><code>bat_animation/
├── bat.py
├── bat.scss
├── modes.py
├── renderer.py
├── scss_parser.py
│
├── legacy_files/
│   ├── bat_simple.py
│   └── entire_bat.py
│
├── shell/
│   ├── bashrc_snippet.sh
│   └── powershell_profile.ps1
│
└── .gitignore</code></pre>


    <h3><code>bat.py</code></h3>

    <p>Main entry point.</p>

    <p>It:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>Parses command-line arguments</li>
        <li>Loads the SCSS animation</li>
        <li>Prepares the rendered frames</li>
        <li>Selects the requested animation mode</li>
        <li>Manages terminal setup and cleanup</li>
    </ul>


    <h3><code>scss_parser.py</code></h3>

    <p>
        Reads <code>bat.scss</code> and extracts the pixel coordinates
        for each animation frame.
    </p>


    <h3><code>renderer.py</code></h3>

    <p>Handles:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>Conversion from pixels to Unicode Braille</li>
        <li>Colors</li>
        <li>Sprite mirroring</li>
        <li>Terminal clipping</li>
        <li>Terminal dimensions</li>
        <li>Drawing frames</li>
    </ul>


    <h3><code>modes.py</code></h3>

    <p>Contains the animation behaviours:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>Normal flight</li>
        <li>Return flight</li>
        <li>Perch</li>
        <li>Swarm</li>
        <li>Dance</li>
    </ul>


    <h3><code>legacy_files/</code></h3>

    <p>
        Contains earlier versions of the program from before the code
        was split into separate modules. As well as a simpler version of it before it evolved.
    </p>


    <hr>


    <h2>How It Works</h2>

    <p>
        The original animation represents the bat using many CSS
        <code>box-shadow</code> coordinates.
    </p>

    <p>A line such as:</p>

    <pre><code>33px 6px $a,</code></pre>

    <p>
        represents one colored pixel.
    </p>

    <p>
        <code>scss_parser.py</code> extracts these coordinates and groups
        them by animation frame.
    </p>

    <p>
        The renderer then compresses the source pixels into Unicode
        Braille cells.
    </p>

    <p>
        A Braille character can represent a grid of:
    </p>

    <pre><code>2 × 4 pixels</code></pre>

    <p>
        which makes it possible to reproduce the pixel-art bat at a
        practical size inside a terminal.
    </p>

    <p>
        The resulting frames are drawn using ANSI cursor positioning
        and color escape sequences.
    </p>


